  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on an exploratory comparative framework of only four case study cities, which limits the immediate generalizability of the regional differences identified.",
      "what": "This study compared the geometric and topological structures of water pipeline and road networks across four cities: Kigali (Rwanda), Kisumu (Kenya), La Rochelle (France), and Vancouver (Canada). Representing the infrastructure as dual graphs and using total least squares analysis, the researchers measured network centrality, global topological properties, and spatial co-location. The analysis revealed that while pipeline networks are highly similar across all four cities, road networks vary by region, with Southern cities showing lower spatial co-location between roads and pipelines and a faster decline in pipeline centrality from the center to the periphery.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that water pipeline structures do not simply mirror road networks, particularly in the Global South where community distribution and water facility locations create distinct spatial patterns. This challenges the common modeling assumption that road networks can serve as a reliable proxy for underground water infrastructure layout across different global contexts."
    }
  },
